WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.656
Applications routinely translate request
data into filesystem operations:

2
00:00:04.736 --> 00:00:08.263
downloading attachments, storing uploads,
generating reports,

3
00:00:08.343 --> 00:00:11.933
selecting templates, serving static
assets, moving tenant files,

4
00:00:12.013 --> 00:00:14.846
creating archives, and cleaning temporary
content.

5
00:00:14.926 --> 00:00:19.272
Path traversal occurs when externally
influenced path information causes one of

6
00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.941
those operations to reach beyond its
authorized storage boundary.

7
00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:25.981
The defect is therefore not merely a
suspicious string.

8
00:00:26.061 --> 00:00:30.469
It is a failed decision about which
filesystem object the application is permitted

9
00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:31.116
to access.

10
00:00:31.196 --> 00:00:35.226
Prepending a trusted directory with
path.join does not prove containment.

11
00:00:35.306 --> 00:00:37.951
Parent components may normalize beyond the
root,

12
00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:40.928
and absolute path syntax, separators,
drive letters,

13
00:00:41.008 --> 00:00:43.716
or network paths have platform-specific
meaning.

14
00:00:43.796 --> 00:00:47.386
path.normalize rewrites lexical syntax; it
does not grant access.

15
00:00:47.466 --> 00:00:51.811
URL and framework decoding can also change
the representation before Node's path

16
00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:52.521
APIs see it.

17
00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:57.072
Searching only for a literal pair of dots
leaves the application reasoning about one

18
00:00:57.152 --> 00:01:00.238
spelling rather than the target derived
from the request.

19
00:01:00.318 --> 00:01:03.907
A stronger design begins with an
operation-specific trusted root.

20
00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:06.191
Treat the client reference as untrusted,

21
00:01:06.271 --> 00:01:10.113
derive an absolute lexical candidate in
the intended platform context,

22
00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:13.531
and compare that candidate with the root
using path semantics.

23
00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:17.074
Then decide whether symbolic links,
junctions, reparse points,

24
00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:21.499
writable directories, or concurrent
changes can redirect the actual filesystem

25
00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:22.020
lookup.

26
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.131
Finally, authorize the requested object
and rely on restricted filesystem

27
00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:28.163
permissions to limit consequences.

28
00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:30.573
Each control answers a different question.

29
00:01:30.653 --> 00:01:34.369
The durable mental model has six steps:
identify the authorized root,

30
00:01:34.449 --> 00:01:37.156
minimize the path syntax accepted from the
client,

31
00:01:37.236 --> 00:01:40.385
resolve the intended target, verify
lexical containment,

32
00:01:40.465 --> 00:01:42.984
account for relevant filesystem
indirection,

33
00:01:43.064 --> 00:01:46.654
and perform an authorized operation with
appropriate privileges.

34
00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:50.134
A harmless-looking original string is not
the security result.

35
00:01:50.214 --> 00:01:54.623
The result is the authorized filesystem
target the operating system will actually

36
00:01:54.703 --> 00:01:56.340
read, create, move, or remove.
